  'Music History III (20th C.-Contemporary)',
  'Music History III (20th C.-Contemporary)',
  'Music History III surveys twentieth-century and contemporary music across modernism, experimentalism, jazz, American music, electronic media, minimalism, popular music, global contemporary traditions, and twenty-first-century practice. The course treats scores, recordings, archives, technologies, institutions, scenes, and media circulation as historical evidence. Students learn to analyze music whose forms may be tonal, atonal, serial, improvised, process-based, electronic, multimedia, popular, global, or community-based. The course asks not only what changed after 1900, but how modern musical histories are made, mediated, contested, and revised.',
  $mh3json${"slug": "music-history-iii-20th-c-contemporary", "title": "Music History III (20th C.-Contemporary)", "description": "Music History III surveys twentieth-century and contemporary music across modernism, experimentalism, jazz, American music, electronic media, minimalism, popular music, global contemporary traditions, and twenty-first-century practice. The course treats scores, recordings, archives, technologies, institutions, scenes, and media circulation as historical evidence. Students learn to analyze music whose forms may be tonal, atonal, serial, improvised, process-based, electronic, multimedia, popular, global, or community-based. The course asks not only what changed after 1900, but how modern musical histories are made, mediated, contested, and revised.", "topics": [{"id": "eca931e9-6c8d-45f4-93a3-082aa998695c", "type": "topic", "title": "Impressionism", "description": "This opening topic teaches students to hear color, ambiguity, symbolist aesthetics, and new relationships between harmony, timbre, form, and atmosphere at the edge of modernism.", "children": [{"id": "1b815131-9611-4948-be99-f7eda8c35ac1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Debussy, Ravel, and French Modernism", "children": [{"id": "238489ef-3339-48d9-baf3-6d3d976c2043", "type": "concept", "title": "Prelude a l'apres-midi d'un faune, La mer, Jeux"}, {"id": "1b8f8f44-9a65-4fbc-8c84-444bb0d5a2f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ravel's orchestration, dance, and historical distance"}, {"id": "456cf7b6-4587-46e7-b61d-1eb0498e54a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolism, salons, colonial exhibition culture, and Parisian institutions"}], "description": "Students learn why Impressionism is useful but also historically contested as a label."}, {"id": "81745a4f-4e3a-4cb8-b7cb-3f8290ebbacf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Harmony, Mode, and Timbre", "children": [{"id": "55515836-c4ac-4e15-bdf7-ad26be14b075", "type": "concept", "title": "Whole-tone, pentatonic, modal, and octatonic materials"}, {"id": "e65e39be-4110-483e-8ebd-e12c176f90e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Non-functional harmony and planing"}, {"id": "371cb741-5113-4c8d-a209-7b91b7d41e17", "type": "concept", "title": "Orchestral color, resonance, and register"}], "description": "The point is to identify musical structures that resist older tonal teleology."}, {"id": "c740e557-67df-4bd0-8164-9223b5a8636a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Form, Listening, and Visual Culture", "children": [{"id": "b7d6b47c-83b5-41f1-b944-8871934ba811", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragmentary form and cyclic memory"}, {"id": "514ddf75-82c2-4a9e-b8e0-afa5f4574094", "type": "concept", "title": "Program, image, and metaphor"}, {"id": "4e908d90-40f9-402a-8b09-0532dd31be71", "type": "concept", "title": "Problems with the term Impressionism"}], "description": "Students connect musical surface to perception and modernist aesthetics."}]}, {"id": "f7c82d2b-ee1c-4450-afa2-2759d78ab4da", "type": "topic", "title": "Expressionism and Atonality", "description": "Expressionism and free atonality introduce music of psychological intensity, fractured syntax, compressed form, and new ideas of musical subjectivity.", "children": [{"id": "689d5509-3ddd-415f-aa12-a85da4fc363b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Schoenberg and Free Atonality", "children": [{"id": "ff23fa7e-bb2a-48e4-a95b-5662c1aa9e48", "type": "concept", "title": "Erwartung, Pierrot lunaire, Op. 11 piano pieces"}, {"id": "d606d3ba-7c87-4e08-9cac-8c6f09d45f56", "type": "concept", "title": "Motivic cells, pitch-class emphasis, and registral design"}, {"id": "ae298258-f63f-49c3-a622-319500688217", "type": "concept", "title": "Sprechstimme and extreme expression"}], "description": "Students learn how composers organized music after loosening tonal hierarchy."}, {"id": "d4dd9610-4723-45ff-b783-44a9613ca2a3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Psychology, Crisis, and Modernity", "children": [{"id": "a127bb9a-4bec-4de9-b578-71e8f502d9b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Freud, alienation, urban modernity, and anxiety"}, {"id": "827d193c-ea63-49cb-9a69-dd7396792727", "type": "concept", "title": "Expressionist theater, painting, and literature"}, {"id": "845d3d11-bbae-4689-8371-894725de9818", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, hysteria narratives, and staged subjectivity"}], "description": "The unit places sound inside early twentieth-century cultural conditions."}, {"id": "92beac1c-7062-47b6-9de7-20a5987bd976", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Analytical Listening Without Tonal Cadence", "children": [{"id": "57fba49d-7b57-4854-af5f-3a8cef725f95", "type": "concept", "title": "Contour, interval, rhythm, timbre, and text"}, {"id": "18237128-13b5-4cd6-8e2e-334366548c37", "type": "concept", "title": "Atonal phrase design"}, {"id": "1c67ea8a-e2a5-46c1-817f-1ed8ddb55da7", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening maps and short-form analysis"}], "description": "Students practice hearing coherence when conventional tonal markers disappear."}]}, {"id": "309d0c2d-6b12-425c-977f-4b28ffddee23", "type": "topic", "title": "Neoclassicism: Stravinsky and Interwar Modernism", "description": "Neoclassicism shows modernism turning toward older forms, restraint, objectivity, irony, and politics after World War I.", "children": [{"id": "0f9b791d-7f78-4d17-a57a-7e1a98c61275", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Stravinsky's Stylistic Turns", "children": [{"id": "cc6f3055-146f-4cc8-8ce9-584d9a95534b", "type": "concept", "title": "The Rite of Spring as prewar rupture"}, {"id": "485b5bac-d72f-4355-b89d-e94f96e3966c", "type": "concept", "title": "Pulcinella, Octet, Symphony of Psalms"}, {"id": "8f028bda-a10a-4fc8-96df-387c4b192486", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhythm, block form, ostinato, and historical allusion"}], "description": "Students learn to compare primitivism, neoclassicism, and serial late style without flattening them."}, {"id": "5f810835-a84d-4f97-aae8-1b974a8fee85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interwar Culture and Politics", "children": [{"id": "d69c3344-b107-49c3-ac75-103b865fffff", "type": "concept", "title": "Paris, Les Six, and anti-Romantic aesthetics"}, {"id": "4c1d46d9-eadd-4886-8a2d-25f7a231b8fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Objectivity, irony, and clean surfaces"}, {"id": "96ffec1b-e90b-4f2f-83a9-d7b8133f4e3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, ballet, recording, and touring"}], "description": "The point is to understand style as a response to war, nationalism, exile, and institutions."}, {"id": "0b4a87fe-ba0d-484f-860d-633189a89443", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reuse, Quotation, and Historical Distance", "children": [{"id": "29027db1-7185-4996-b2c9-e87b531c5c74", "type": "concept", "title": "Baroque and Classical forms"}, {"id": "7afb0289-f9bd-49fd-bb76-c5ad879defb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastiche, parody, and recomposition"}, {"id": "4103df10-a653-4c9b-9504-6fafc3cfade3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of borrowing and source transparency"}], "description": "Students study how old materials become modern."}]}, {"id": "9d531a55-2491-4016-97fd-f63e9d4114d1", "type": "topic", "title": "The Second Viennese School", "description": "This unit follows Schoenberg, Berg, and Webern from atonality into twelve-tone and serial thought, while asking how method, expression, and reception interact.", "children": [{"id": "48dfbcff-4db3-414e-9c6c-95a73889a63f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Twelve-Tone Technique", "children": [{"id": "cb045048-c437-4399-b865-d14fe25aff95", "type": "concept", "title": "Rows, prime/inversion/retrograde forms"}, {"id": "c1cccfd5-f0fe-4887-9af9-5fdf74b78ebb", "type": "concept", "title": "Hexachords, invariance, and combinatoriality"}, {"id": "6e4f2de4-6c78-4b85-87c5-9133a8cf6f0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Serial procedure and expressive flexibility"}], "description": "Students learn the system as a compositional tool, not a dry arithmetic exercise."}, {"id": "a6ba6f35-8ee5-4a40-bb21-4e72565633c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Berg and Webern", "children": [{"id": "7d726b5b-c743-4471-b06d-f88bf0248420", "type": "concept", "title": "Berg's lyricism, opera, and tonal reference"}, {"id": "c0e5cf46-ad04-4e0d-a913-e78bbc5cf353", "type": "concept", "title": "Webern's compression, timbre, and pointillism"}, {"id": "ef590b7f-fdec-44f7-b323-5df51f39e340", "type": "concept", "title": "Chamber scale, silence, and concentration"}], "description": "The unit compares very different responses to shared technique."}, {"id": "f30e6b0b-2f25-43e8-82fa-83c66be4bab0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postwar Reception and Canon", "children": [{"id": "cfd57dc8-0ce5-41d6-a59f-f0aebca58da4", "type": "concept", "title": "Darmstadt and academic modernism"}, {"id": "5560cfa7-dd1b-4357-9837-7db2f63b57b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Cold War cultural politics"}, {"id": "e06d6f79-a695-4933-bdab-1096c520d638", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of difficulty, elitism, and exclusion"}], "description": "Students examine how serialism became institutionally powerful after 1945."}]}, {"id": "0089afe7-8b9a-4bef-a615-5f4e6ad4842b", "type": "topic", "title": "American Music: Copland, Ives, and Beyond", "description": "American modernisms are presented as plural: experimental, vernacular, nationalist, Black, Indigenous, immigrant, populist, academic, and commercial.", "children": [{"id": "dd4025ba-e53a-4702-b8c4-e0609cf00093", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ives, Experimentalism, and Memory", "children": [{"id": "bd97c804-2fe4-4aa5-bf23-f10d336f991f", "type": "concept", "title": "The Unanswered Question, Concord Sonata, symphonies"}, {"id": "7da1848e-d8ec-4648-986a-581e5ad59172", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation, layering, and amateur/professional tensions"}, {"id": "e7cce8df-105d-4842-b9f5-330f2f5d9e29", "type": "concept", "title": "Transcendentalism and New England memory"}], "description": "Students hear collage, quotation, hymnody, and simultaneity as historical imagination."}, {"id": "15f7acc7-57a8-4594-8944-70d395676a63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Copland, Still, Crawford Seeger, and Public Style", "children": [{"id": "45b70014-513f-4d36-8f91-1ee16b2990ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Copland's populist style and ballet/film work"}, {"id": "94a2ab9f-93f5-48f4-95a9-a3ba3bd2d38f", "type": "concept", "title": "William Grant Still and the Harlem Renaissance"}, {"id": "379440d5-acfc-4b6f-b631-bd3b23828dde", "type": "concept", "title": "Ruth Crawford Seeger, ultramodernism, and folk work"}], "description": "The unit broadens the American canon while studying institutions and identity."}, {"id": "80c6b98b-68ac-4646-be9a-53181f8b34d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutions, Media, and Nation", "children": [{"id": "5e86d5f6-61ba-4a50-87eb-73a51d19cefa", "type": "concept", "title": "New Deal arts programs"}, {"id": "e793e609-61e5-4af7-adc8-e69a88f77421", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservatories, orchestras, and publishing"}, {"id": "8b2b4127-ffee-4ce2-8534-4ff36cdd67c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, immigration, regionalism, and access"}], "description": "Students connect style to funding, education, radio, film, and public culture."}]}, {"id": "cf653ae0-5613-4f8b-847b-ce3d8f909aa8", "type": "topic", "title": "Jazz and Its Influence", "description": "Jazz is studied as a major art form with its own histories, practices, technologies, and communities, not merely as influence on concert music.", "children": [{"id": "699cc8d8-ae5f-48ff-a555-44a7abb7ef5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Blues, Swing, Bebop, and Form", "children": [{"id": "09c8d7f6-87b0-4422-910b-0a09929c20d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Blues form, syncopation, swing feel, and improvisation"}, {"id": "cc67d9af-82c2-4815-b0bd-60872fca4456", "type": "concept", "title": "Ellington, Armstrong, Holiday, Parker, Gillespie, Monk"}, {"id": "3309fd6e-f651-4296-b10e-debc103cbeca", "type": "concept", "title": "Arrangement, head-solo-head, and harmonic rhythm"}], "description": "Students learn core musical materials and historical shifts."}, {"id": "8f880572-5b05-4762-9b32-0b4dcfe636e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Jazz and Concert Music", "children": [{"id": "6d7d211c-ffee-4c62-872e-36fcc91a7fe4", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhapsody in Blue, jazz concerto, Third Stream"}, {"id": "5b5bf7cf-6b99-4ad3-911c-ab5b440389df", "type": "concept", "title": "Milhaud, Stravinsky, Bernstein, and Ellington"}, {"id": "21bd09e3-d3ea-4667-99a9-482b1a0215d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Appropriation, collaboration, and audience framing"}], "description": "The unit treats crossing between scenes critically and musically."}, {"id": "dfb6437d-c4d3-4ddf-8cab-b0fcf7d9e012", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Industry, and Media", "children": [{"id": "5f2a1987-1397-483a-87f5-b232342be718", "type": "concept", "title": "Recording industry and radio"}, {"id": "c4322d3a-d1e4-4de6-86bf-924a04920554", "type": "concept", "title": "Segregation, touring circuits, and labor"}, {"id": "6cb8dfd1-e287-4e40-94f0-80dda6cdc3c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism, canon formation, and Black modernism"}], "description": "Students examine the social systems that shaped jazz circulation."}]}, {"id": "900ad8da-a71a-4d94-81fb-47c5abb74ead", "type": "topic", "title": "Mid-Century Experimentation: Cage and After", "description": "Postwar experimental music challenges the definitions of composition, notation, performance, sound, silence, and audience attention.", "children": [{"id": "a237f34e-8978-4f7e-b45e-c5c97bf58819", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cage, Indeterminacy, and Silence", "children": [{"id": "9e876246-de17-4e34-a610-0adba4f35537", "type": "concept", "title": "4'33'', Music of Changes, prepared piano"}, {"id": "3a84a5e3-1dba-4c61-b719-b1cce3b4c641", "type": "concept", "title": "I Ching, chance operations, and indeterminacy"}, {"id": "158aa52f-55ce-4a57-a376-f42f6e82e991", "type": "concept", "title": "Silence, environment, and attention"}], "description": "Students learn how chance and listening became compositional materials."}, {"id": "1126c2a3-7e14-4266-93a5-bc07c4c495a1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experimental Notation and Performance", "children": [{"id": "9e4e8c14-2636-4652-a151-4ed94306677e", "type": "concept", "title": "Graphic notation and open form"}, {"id": "f7eb54b9-658d-4bb3-b0ca-d02f21759a6b", "type": "concept", "title": "Fluxus, happenings, and performance art"}, {"id": "c3a7b6f8-b852-4e95-bc76-d6ab899f4f61", "type": "concept", "title": "Performer agency and ethics"}], "description": "The point is to understand scores as invitations, systems, and events."}, {"id": "172c867b-6e8d-4c98-8354-3ddc792a95fd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sound Art and Expanded Listening", "children": [{"id": "a8caacd6-c668-4ec7-bf6e-117a8f0f9be2", "type": "concept", "title": "Murray Schafer, soundscape, and acoustic ecology"}, {"id": "53d80b1a-db00-4d15-a6bd-03e35fc1b6fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Pauline Oliveros and deep listening"}, {"id": "7436ea96-522c-4bd2-b4d5-e9e59398a95a", "type": "concept", "title": "Technology, space, and embodied attention"}], "description": "Students connect music history to galleries, installations, and everyday sound."}]}, {"id": "2e9f3f92-7196-4667-b77c-b19f3a1f459a", "type": "topic", "title": "Minimalism", "description": "Minimalism centers process, repetition, pulse, gradual change, perception, spirituality, technology, and new relationships between concert, popular, and global practices.", "children": [{"id": "4b68ccc5-df6d-4ebe-9284-bb3f13784a0b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early Minimalist Processes", "children": [{"id": "26cc133e-28cf-420e-baeb-336486dd6436", "type": "concept", "title": "La Monte Young, Terry Riley, Steve Reich, Philip Glass"}, {"id": "5b172f3f-05e6-4abd-9b9f-cc0b3248a23f", "type": "concept", "title": "Drones, phasing, additive process, and pulse"}, {"id": "86194d24-114e-4f07-a5ce-bf0b463b0e42", "type": "concept", "title": "Tape loops, ensemble discipline, and duration"}], "description": "Students learn to hear process as form."}, {"id": "2be2245f-854a-419c-a029-90677b7a829d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postminimalism and Listening Culture", "children": [{"id": "f1ff279f-b24f-4cb5-b05b-6dfa864d61eb", "type": "concept", "title": "John Adams, Julia Wolfe, Michael Gordon, David Lang"}, {"id": "087a2e87-0e9f-4d6d-bb82-1cc9aa4a5f06", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility, criticism, and institutional adoption"}, {"id": "8af273e1-f60d-4dd2-af8b-0c498912fdaf", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimalism in film, dance, and multimedia"}], "description": "The unit follows minimalism into later concert, opera, film, and popular circulation."}, {"id": "fa8a0cbf-ee4e-4bd6-8c60-5b7c8d4f73df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Influence, Borrowing, and Global Sound", "children": [{"id": "ab28228d-7f10-4803-b77c-add72032adbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian classical music, West African drumming, Balinese gamelan references"}, {"id": "96dd0433-6fa3-4c73-b028-757827f9d62a", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of influence and mediation"}, {"id": "b415bd72-1a89-432c-a780-155a07e8a89d", "type": "concept", "title": "Repetition in electronic dance and popular music"}], "description": "Students ask how minimalism interacted with non-Western traditions and popular media."}]}, {"id": "42ce09e2-b2fb-4ec7-abb7-e7235a85cb65", "type": "topic", "title": "Electronic and Computer Music", "description": "This topic treats technology as musical material, workplace, instrument, archive, and historical force.", "children": [{"id": "b5c906d9-2b89-413e-86f1-c0be569ad1ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tape, Studio, and Synthesizer", "children": [{"id": "558be4d9-5e16-4a84-a758-2f3b70227015", "type": "concept", "title": "Musique concrete, elektronische Musik, Columbia-Princeton, BBC Radiophonic Workshop"}, {"id": "ce4fef7f-5530-4347-8a63-1f9d42fdebc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Tape splicing, looping, filtering, and spatialization"}, {"id": "12615f1e-593b-4198-aab8-5b1cbd5145e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Synthesizers, voltage control, and early studios"}], "description": "Students trace how fixed media changed composition and listening."}, {"id": "993927df-0f9d-4566-a05f-aa210a62dbc7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Computer Music and Algorithmic Thought", "children": [{"id": "a579bee3-a684-4b8d-9957-2a186b5e6788", "type": "concept", "title": "Max/MSP, SuperCollider, algorithmic composition"}, {"id": "18ef3d3f-e57b-4424-8a84-ffc234ebfa14", "type": "concept", "title": "MIDI, sampling, DAWs, and digital audio"}, {"id": "5fc75462-cddb-4105-be7c-b65acf53f93e", "type": "concept", "title": "Spectralism, analysis/resynthesis, and timbre"}], "description": "The unit connects composition to code, systems, and machine listening."}, {"id": "86c6c80c-e590-4aa7-8308-cdbef1e47395", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media, Labor, and Access", "children": [{"id": "b5503bf0-d909-4bdb-ad62-64637731e193", "type": "concept", "title": "Studio institutions and funding"}, {"id": "6629db1d-d323-417f-acda-f7f3d8d0f821", "type": "concept", "title": "DIY electronics, laptop performance, and live coding"}, {"id": "21c2f746-5870-4c12-b8b1-76238c0132a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Copyright, sampling, platforms, and archives"}], "description": "Students examine who gets to use technology and how tools shape aesthetics."}]}, {"id": "3782f334-aa49-4fb0-b882-bf010f80050b", "type": "topic", "title": "Music of the 21st Century", "description": "Contemporary music is approached through living composers, global scenes, multimedia, identity, climate, opera, installation, streaming, and the collapse of easy genre boundaries.", "children": [{"id": "6569895e-9518-44b9-977e-1ce81a2bcf05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Living Composers and New Music Scenes", "children": [{"id": "6e9e87d9-cd01-4dcf-a4b6-784cc24343ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Caroline Shaw, Tyshawn Sorey, Unsuk Chin, Kaija Saariaho, Du Yun, Gabriela Ortiz"}, {"id": "5ea74600-6351-4fc6-a0c3-2c782fe2cd71", "type": "concept", "title": "Ensembles, festivals, commissions, and residencies"}, {"id": "0af0d10d-e22b-42e8-883e-56f346f10902", "type": "concept", "title": "Notation, improvisation, and hybrid practice"}], "description": "Students learn to study contemporary work through interviews, programs, recordings, and institutional context."}, {"id": "bbcd33d8-72a2-41da-a7db-2616a0c80ebb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multimedia, Opera, and Installation", "children": [{"id": "76bb51a9-d3c1-4f30-bbd4-78f9162f8e64", "type": "concept", "title": "New opera and music theater"}, {"id": "07ceefe3-fe84-4d29-ab54-7499fc1aac04", "type": "concept", "title": "Video, installation, and immersive sound"}, {"id": "a333a373-18ba-4514-8da6-d8e15249887f", "type": "concept", "title": "Interdisciplinary collaboration"}], "description": "The unit connects music to stage, screen, space, and image."}, {"id": "9341dcf6-2217-4eca-9c46-9a19ddf0628e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Identity, Ecology, and Public Life", "children": [{"id": "ed371fc2-ca83-44a8-b7a7-5b7bc28b7370", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, gender, sexuality, disability, migration"}, {"id": "47fe482b-ff49-44b5-899b-0f995ee31ac6", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate and environmental sound work"}, {"id": "3065eee9-a4e2-4d7c-8d5d-4dd8fc74216a", "type": "concept", "title": "Community practice and public humanities"}], "description": "Students consider how recent music addresses urgent social questions."}]}, {"id": "1de97b61-ef41-43b2-bd4f-ff00fc49beba", "type": "topic", "title": "Global Contemporary Traditions, Popular Music, Recording, and Media Circulation", "description": "Students study music history through global circulation, diaspora, recording, popular culture, streaming, and decolonizing methods.", "children": [{"id": "c2d6c8b5-b028-4ea4-a2d7-9b4b7239a292", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global and Diasporic Modernisms", "children": [{"id": "df2aa924-9bc8-41dd-b98e-8b975816489b", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American, African, East Asian, South Asian, Middle Eastern, and Indigenous modernisms"}, {"id": "646c602a-2817-4ac2-a738-9bd3be525b9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora, migration, and transnational collaboration"}, {"id": "0c2b91c6-1d2d-4c38-9f57-5458ce377b5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Postcolonial and decolonial perspectives"}], "description": "Students learn that modern/contemporary music is not a single European-American story."}, {"id": "e9cdf906-564f-4f89-af8f-f09ad27a0a29", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Popular Music and Media Industries", "children": [{"id": "0308b2d9-b725-4fb2-8cb6-0a2049ebc176", "type": "concept", "title": "Rock, hip-hop, EDM, film music, game music, and music video"}, {"id": "98ffa646-a0e2-4ae2-81ca-083c5e97185f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling, remix, production, and beat-making"}, {"id": "2fe71da5-5d8b-4b81-9978-6376d41cad47", "type": "concept", "title": "Charts, labels, streaming platforms, and fan cultures"}], "description": "The unit studies popular music as historical evidence and artistic practice."}, {"id": "e99b24c8-ea8d-41ee-a33d-f8373aa8ae64", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Recording, Circulation, and Archives", "children": [{"id": "74e0ede9-63cc-4dbc-9fdd-bd4ddca400d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Phonograph, radio, LP, cassette, CD, MP3, streaming"}, {"id": "d6233096-b28c-4f64-8593-d2b756b7b292", "type": "concept", "title": "Field recordings, ethnography, and archival ethics"}, {"id": "51b25b9e-e243-4487-b380-9cd2d734206c", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform power, algorithms, and datafication"}], "description": "Students ask how media changes what music is and how history remembers it."}]}, {"id": "9eccbda6-9068-4a65-a1ed-a982975e4e70", "type": "topic", "title": "Methods for Contemporary Music History", "description": "This topic gives students tools for researching music whose sources may be scores, recordings, code, interviews, social media, performances, archives, or communities.", "children": [{"id": "b4db1504-c6cc-48b5-92fd-390e1448c256", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Listening and Analysis Across Media", "children": [{"id": "bea72583-c203-4aab-8a78-6708f5d6f8da", "type": "concept", "title": "Score analysis, spectrograms, transcription, and close listening"}, {"id": "970a5c54-13c4-461d-86cb-3efa41338413", "type": "concept", "title": "Production analysis and sound studies"}, {"id": "66db56f4-b341-4708-a530-2e641cf06ac6", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodied, social, and ecological listening"}], "description": "Students learn to choose analytical tools that fit the music."}, {"id": "692c023e-cc7d-4047-b414-13a6bb5fd12c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primary Sources and Field Methods", "children": [{"id": "ecdfe658-0189-4bdf-bf45-c5cf99bd0c69", "type": "concept", "title": "Interviews, oral history, program notes, reviews, and digital traces"}, {"id": "3b5eabaa-20d9-464b-88b2-1efac75dfc3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics, consent, representation, and positionality"}, {"id": "04e3481b-79c1-4436-a07a-a96981f26931", "type": "concept", "title": "Archiving ephemeral and online materials"}], "description": "The unit trains students to work responsibly with living cultures and recent histories."}, {"id": "4638505b-c3a5-4365-9281-8f5832a4966d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historiography and Curriculum Repair", "children": [{"id": "9cafa1ae-8100-4dc8-b3a3-1590332e5694", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernism, postmodernism, and period labels"}, {"id": "266d6ed0-c4ed-4512-b3d1-df65385d1668", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon gaps and institutional power"}, {"id": "005f2bed-2aa4-422a-a68c-502d3bad0ecc", "type": "concept", "title": "Designing inclusive listening lists and research questions"}], "description": "Students critique survey narratives and build better ones."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$mh3json$::jsonb,
